course: myths
lesson: 9
title: Small creatures and creepy-crawlies
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Charlie stands in the garden with a spade, grinning: "Cut a worm in half and you get TWO worms. Free worm!" Lottie's brother whispers at bedtime that earwigs crawl into your ears at night and munch your brain.
explain: Minibeasts are small, fast and strange, so we make up stories about them, and the scarier the story, the faster it spreads round the playground.
explain: Most of the stories fall apart when you look closely: a cut worm usually loses its tail end, earwigs got their spooky name from old folk beliefs, and daddy longlegs are harmless to people.
explain: Some fall apart when you simply count: a spider has eight legs and two body parts, so it isn't an insect, and a seven-spot ladybird has seven spots its whole life, so spots aren't birthdays.
explain: When you want to settle an argument about a creature, read a guide, count, or watch; never hurt an animal to prove a point.
fact: If you cut an earthworm in half, usually only the head end may survive and grow a new tail; the tail end dies, though some flatworms can regrow from pieces.
fact: None of the creatures called "daddy longlegs" is dangerous to people; cellar spiders' venom is weak for us, they very rarely bite, and they even eat other spiders.
fact: Warts are caused by a human virus, so toads can't give you warts; some toads have skin that irritates, so wash your hands after touching one.
fact: Only honeybees usually die after stinging people, because their barbed sting sticks in our skin; bumblebees and wasps can sting more than once.
fact: Earwigs don't seek out ears and can't eat brains; their name probably comes from old folk beliefs.
fact: House flies live about two to four weeks; it is adult mayflies that may live only hours or a day.
fact: Spiders are arachnids, with eight legs, two body parts and no antennae; insects have six legs and three body parts.
fact: Adult clothes moths don't eat clothes; their caterpillars eat wool and other natural fibres.
fact: A ladybird's number of spots usually shows its kind, not its age; a seven-spot ladybird always has seven spots.
fact: Ants can lift many times their own weight, but a giant ant would collapse, because when a body gets bigger its weight grows much faster than the strength of its legs.
fact: Some snails go dormant (shut up still, as if in a deep sleep) for long periods in dry weather, in rare cases even years, waiting for damp conditions.
exercise: Charlie says cutting a worm in half gives two worms. What usually happens? | Only the head end may survive and grow a new tail | Both halves wriggle away and become two happy, healthy worms | Both halves die at once, always | The tail end grows a new head
exercise: Which kind of worm really can regrow from pieces? | Some flatworms | Every earthworm | Only worms in water | No worm at all
exercise: Dylan says daddy longlegs are the most poisonous spiders but their fangs are too short. What's true? | They are harmless to people, and rarely bite at all | They are deadly, but their fangs can't bite through skin | They are the most poisonous creature on the whole Earth | They crawl into beds and bite people every single night
exercise: Grace's nan says toads give you warts. What causes warts? | A virus that passes between people | Touching the dry, bumpy skin of a toad | Eating frog spawn out of a garden pond | Sitting for too long on cold, damp grass
exercise: Why should you still wash your hands after holding a toad? | Some toads have skin that can irritate | Toads always pass on warts to anyone who picks them up | Toads leave invisible glue | Toads carry the cold virus
exercise: Ahmed says bees always die after they sting you. Which insect usually does? | A honeybee | A wasp | A bumblebee | Every stinging insect
exercise: Why does a honeybee usually die after stinging a person? | Its barbed sting gets stuck in our skin | It runs out of its poison and can never make more | It is too frightened to go on living afterwards | It forgets how to fly after it has used its sting
exercise: Lottie's brother says earwigs crawl into your ears and eat your brain. What's true? | They don't seek out ears and can't eat brains | They do it, but only at night | They eat ear wax every evening | They live inside most people's ears
exercise: Freddie says a fly lives only 24 hours. How long does a house fly live? | About two to four weeks | Exactly one day | About ten or twenty years | One hour
exercise: Which insect's adults may live only hours to a day? | Mayflies | House flies | Ants | Ladybirds
exercise: Rosie puts spiders on her insect chart. Why is that wrong? | Spiders have eight legs and two body parts; insects have six and three | Spiders have six legs like beetles | Spiders are reptiles | Spiders can fly but insects can't
exercise: Mum finds holes in a jumper and blames the moths flying about. What actually eats the wool? | The moth caterpillars | The grown-up moths | Woodlice in the wardrobe | Spiders hunting the moths
exercise: Nana says a ladybird's spots tell its age. What do the spots usually show? | Which kind of ladybird it is | How many birthdays it has had | How many babies it has | How hungry it is
exercise: Ollie says an ant as big as a human could lift a car. Why not? | Its weight would grow much faster than its leg strength, so it would collapse | It would be too shy to try | Cars are made of a metal ants can't grip | It could, ants never get weaker
exercise: A viral post says snails can sleep for three years. How true is it? | Partly: some snails go dormant for a long time waiting for damp | Completely false, snails never rest | True, every single snail sleeps for exactly three years at a time | True, but only in the sea
exercise: What's the best way to settle an argument about whether a worm survives being cut? | Read a guide, and don't hurt a worm to find out | Cut one in half to see | Ask the worm | Cut lots of worms in half and count how many grow back
test: "Saw a daddy longlegs in the bath. MOST POISONOUS SPIDER, get out!!" What do you say? | It's harmless to us; just leave it, it eats other spiders | Run away fast, because just one bite from it is deadly to people | Spray it, it bites everyone | It can't bite but it can sting
test: A wasp keeps buzzing near your lunch. Your friend says, "Relax, it'll die if it stings." What's wrong? | Wasps can sting more than once; walk away calmly | Nothing, because wasps always die after they sting you | Wasps can't sting at all, they only buzz to scare people | Wasps only ever sting people in the cold winter months
test: You find a creature with six legs, three body parts and antennae. What is it? | An insect | A spider | A worm | A snail
test: A seven-spot ladybird lands on you in June and another in September. How many spots do they have? | Seven each, the spots don't grow with age | Seven in June, and then more of them by September | Fewer in September, because their spots fall off | It depends on how warm the summer has been
test: In a superhero film, a man shrinks to ant size and keeps his strength... no wait, an ant grows to human size and lifts a bus. What's the science problem? | Bigger bodies get much heavier, so the giant ant would collapse | Ants are too lazy to lift buses | Buses are too smooth for ants | No problem at all, because a giant ant would be exactly as strong
test: "Grandad's got a wart. Bet he touched a toad!" What's the better explanation? | Warts come from a human virus | Toads give warts only to grandads | He must have eaten a frog | Warts come from cold weather
test: Your mum buys moth balls and swats every flying moth to save her jumpers. What would help more? | Looking for and dealing with the caterpillars in the wool | Swatting more flying moths | Wearing the jumpers in the rain | Putting the jumpers out in the bright sun for just one minute
test: An earwig is on your pillow. Your little brother is screaming. What do you tell him? | Earwigs don't go for ears; it's just a spooky name | Hide your ears, it's coming for your brain | Earwigs only eat the brains of children who sleep on their side | Put cotton wool in your ears forever
test: A snail stuck to a dry wall all summer suddenly moves after rain. What happened? | It was dormant, waiting for damp weather | It was dead and came back to life | It was glued there by a bird | It had been sleeping for three years exactly
test: Freddie and Kit argue: Freddie says flies live a day, Kit says weeks. How could they settle it? | Look up house flies and mayflies separately in an encyclopaedia | Catch a fly and keep it in a jar | Toss a coin | Whoever shouts loudest wins
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the anatomy of a spider? | It possesses eight legs, two body regions and no antennae | It possesses six legs, three body regions, a pair of antennae and wings | It possesses eight legs, three body regions and wings | It possesses six legs and no distinct body regions
exam: Identify the principal cause of warts upon human skin. | A virus transmitted between persons | Contact with the skin of a toad | Prolonged exposure to damp conditions | Consumption of amphibians
exam: It is given that an earthworm has been divided into two parts. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | At most the head end may survive and regenerate a tail | Both parts invariably develop into complete worms | The tail end invariably develops a new head | Both parts invariably survive without regeneration
exam: Ascertain which stage of the clothes moth is responsible for damage to woollen garments. | The larval stage, commonly termed the caterpillar | The adult winged moth | The egg prior to hatching | The pupal stage, while it is still within its silken cocoon
